<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span><span id="A1.T5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human-likeness beyond <span id="A1.T5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Soul</span>-Index (HumT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; <math id="A1.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 100</annotation></semantics></math>, higher is more human-like).</span>
Per-text anthropomorphism scalar—the log-prob ratio of animate vs. inanimate prefixes under a fixed GPT-2 backbone—measured on HumT’s held-out prompts, a signal entirely outside the <span id="A1.T5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Soul</span>-Index training objective.
<span id="A1.T5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Others</span> is the best prior human-simulation model. <span id="A1.T5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> marks the best entry.</figcaption>
